ABSTRACT
Employee attrition prediction is fundamentally a class-imbalanced classification problem in which minority-class
performance governs operational value, rather than overall accuracy. This research presents a rigorous machine
```
learning pipeline for predicting employee attrition on the IBM HR Analytics dataset (1,470 employees, 16.12%
```
```
attrition). Six base classifiers, two ensemble strategies, and four boosted ensemble combinations are evaluated
```
under a strictly leakage-free protocol in which all preprocessing, SMOTE, and feature selection are embedded
inside the cross-validation loop, and an untouched 30% holdout is reserved for final evaluation. Using repeated
```
stratified 5-fold cross-validation (5×5), mean±SD is reported for ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, F1, recall, precision,
```
```
balanced accuracy, and MCC. Surprisingly, Logistic Regression emerged as the champion model (F1 = 52.41%,
```
```
Recall = 53.52%), outperforming complex ensembles such as Stacking (F1 = 45.31%). Statistical testing revealed
```
```
no significant difference between the Stacking Ensemble and the strongest baseline (DeLong p = 0.786, McNe-
```
```
mar p = 0.238). Tuning-objective sensitivity is examined across five scoring objectives, and validation-based
```
