1. Introduction
Employee attrition represents one of the most consequential workforce challenges facing modern organizations.
The financial implications are substantial: replacing an employee can cost between 50 and 200% of annual salary
when accounting for recruitment, onboarding, training, and lost productivity [1]. High attrition rates also dis-
rupt team dynamics, deplete institutional knowledge, and place additional strain on remaining employees [2].
These effects are especially pronounced in knowledge-intensive industries where specialized skills take months to
replace.
Traditional HR approaches to attrition have been largely reactive, focusing on exit interviews and post-departure
analysis [3]. Advances in human resource information systems and machine learning now enable a shift toward
predictive analytics, identifying at-risk employees before they resign and enabling proactive retention interven-
tions [4]. This transition from descriptive to predictive HR analytics represents a paradigm shift in workforce
management [5].
This research addresses employee attrition prediction as a binary classification problem using the IBM HR Ana-
lytics Employee Attrition dataset. Only 16.12% of employees in the sample left the organization, creating a severe
class imbalance that renders traditional accuracy-based evaluation misleading [6]. A naive classifier predicting
that everyone stays achieves 83.88% accuracy while identifying zero at-risk employees, producing a statistically
impressive but operationally useless result.
Recent literature has explored various machine learning approaches for attrition prediction, yet several unresolved
challenges persist [7]. Many studies prioritize accuracy over recall despite asymmetric error costs, default to the
0.50 threshold without justification [8], use single train/test splits that provide weak evidence for model superiority,
1
and report ROC-AUC as their primary metric even though ROC-AUC can remain high under severe class imbal-
ance while minority-class performance is weak [26]. Feature engineering grounded in HR domain knowledge is
often asserted rather than ablated [11]. Comparative studies that include modern gradient boosting implementa-
tions alongside traditional models remain limited [10], and statistically rigorous model comparison with paired
bootstrap or DeLong tests is rare. Furthermore, fairness auditing across demographic and occupational subgroups
is frequently omitted.
This paper addresses these gaps through a comprehensive, methodologically rigorous pipeline. The main contri-
butions are:
1. A leakage-free, cross-validation-based pipeline is designed, in which SMOTE, feature selection, and hyperpa-
rameter tuning are embedded inside every training fold.
2. A repeated stratified 5×5 cross-validation protocol is employed, reporting mean±SD for ROC-AUC, PR-AUC,
F1, recall, precision, balanced accuracy, and MCC, with an untouched holdout for final confirmation.
3. PR-AUC is adopted as a primary ranking metric alongside ROC-AUC, with classification and decision metrics
reported separately.
4. An objective-function sensitivity study is conducted, demonstrating that the choice of tuning objective (ROC-
```
AUC, PR-AUC, F1, recall, or balanced accuracy) materially changes minority-class performance.
```
5. Statistical model comparison is performed using DeLong tests, paired bootstrap confidence intervals, and Mc-
Nemar tests, distinguishing the best pipeline model from the best extended model.
6. A cost-sensitive decision framework and decision-curve analysis are developed across FN:FP ratios from 1:1
to 10:1, replacing the arbitrary composite scorecard of earlier drafts.
7. Validation-based threshold selection and calibration analysis are performed, together with a subgroup fairness
evaluation revealing severe disparities across job levels and age groups.
The organization of this paper is as follows. Section 2 provides a systematic review of the related literature. Section
3 describes the dataset, the leakage-free pipeline architecture, the preprocessing and feature-engineering steps, the
six base classifiers, the hyperparameter tuning procedure, the ensemble strategies, and the evaluation metrics. Sec-
tion 4 presents the experimental results, beginning with descriptive statistics and attrition distribution, followed
by ablation studies, baseline model performance under repeated cross-validation, objective-function sensitivity,
ensemble performance with statistical significance testing, cost-sensitive model evaluation, threshold optimiza-
tion with calibration and decision-curve analysis, empirical performance boundary analysis, error analysis, and
subgroup fairness analysis. Section 5 discusses the principal findings and their implications, compares the results
with prior work, and outlines limitations and generalizability considerations. Section 6 concludes the paper and
identifies directions for future research.
2. Related Work
The application of machine learning to employee attrition prediction has received significant attention. This
section reviews key developments and identifies the research gap.
2.1. Traditional Machine Learning Approaches
Fallucchi et al. [3] applied multiple machine learning techniques to forecast employee attrition, demonstrating
the viability of predictive analytics in HR contexts. Alghamdi et al. [4] found that ensemble methods generally
outperformed single classifiers. Jain et al. [6] compared logistic regression, decision trees, and random forests on
the IBM HR dataset, showing that tree-based methods capture nonlinear feature interactions but often at the cost
of interpretability.
2.2. Class Imbalance Handling
Jadhav et al. [12] demonstrated the efficacy of SMOTE on imbalanced classification. Chawla et al. [13] originally
```
introduced SMOTE; subsequent work by He and Garcia [14] and Fern´andez et al. [15] extended it with ADASYN,
```
2
Borderline-SMOTE, and SMOTE-Tomek. Critically, SMOTE must be applied inside the cross-validation loop to
prevent synthetic-sample leakage into validation folds [16].
2.3. Neural Network and Deep Learning Approaches
Krishna and Sidharth [7] investigated neural network-based classification, achieving 88% accuracy. Yahia et al.
[10] explored big-data and deep-learning approaches, highlighting scalability advantages. However, deep-learning
approaches typically require substantially larger datasets than the IBM HR dataset provides [17].
2.4. Feature Engineering and Domain Knowledge
Sharma et al. [11] proposed combining fuzzy inference with ensemble machine learning. Fareed et al. [9]
identified overtime, income, and job satisfaction as significant predictors. Marler and Boudreau [18] argue that
HR-specific domain features outperform purely statistical ones, a claim directly tested by the ablation study in
Section 4.2.
2.5. Ensemble Methods
Govindarajan et al. [1] compared Random Forest, SVM, Decision Tree, Gradient Boosting, and a Hybrid Model,
```
achieving 95.0% accuracy. Breiman [19] introduced Random Forests; Friedman [20] developed Gradient Boost-
