3.1.5. Empirical Performance Boundary
Three dataset properties limit attainable performance: feature incompleteness, temporal aggregation, and non-
deterministic outcomes. Feature incompleteness occurs because external market conditions, personal circum-
stances, and compensation relative to market rates are not captured in the HR database. Temporal aggregation
arises because the dataset represents a static snapshot, missing dynamic factors such as recent team dynamics,
managerial conflict, or changing personal obligations. Non-deterministic outcomes occur because employees with
identical recorded feature profiles sometimes exhibit opposite attrition decisions, suggesting that unmeasured vari-
```
ables (e.g., a sudden external job offer) influence the decision. These sources of predictive uncertainty impose an
```
```
empirical performance boundary (estimated at approximately 89.34% accuracy for this dataset), meaning that no
```
model can achieve perfect prediction using the current feature set. Throughout this paper, the observed upper
performance bound is referred to as an empirical performance boundary, rather than as a theoretical ceiling.
