3.8. Base Machine Learning Models
3.8.1. Logistic Regression
Logistic Regression is a linear model that estimates the probability of a binary outcome using the sigmoid function,
```
P (y = 1|x) = 11+e−(β0+βT x) . It models the log-odds of the target as a linear combination of the input features. The
```
```
coefficients (β) represent the change in log-odds for a one-unit increase in the corresponding feature, holding all
```
others constant. Despite its simplicity, LR is highly interpretable and often serves as a robust baseline, particularly
```
when regularized (L1/L2) to prevent overfitting on high-dimensional data [28]. In this study, L2 regularization is
```
used to penalize large coefficients.
3.8.2. Random Forest
```
Random Forest is a bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating) ensemble of decision trees [19]. It constructs multiple
```
decision trees during training, where each tree is grown on a random bootstrap sample of the data, and at each
```
split, only a random subset of features is considered. This double randomization (bagging and feature subspace)
```
decorrelates the individual trees. The final prediction is obtained by averaging the probabilistic predictions of
all trees. By aggregating the predictions of decorrelated trees, Random Forest significantly reduces variance and
8
mitigates the risk of overfitting compared to a single decision tree, while effectively capturing non-linear feature
interactions.
3.8.3. XGBoost
```
Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) is an advanced implementation of gradient-boosted decision trees [21].
```
Unlike Random Forest, which builds trees independently, XGBoost builds trees sequentially, where each new
```
tree is trained to correct the residual errors (pseudo-residuals) of the previous ensemble. XGBoost minimizes a
```
```
regularized objective function that includes both a loss term (e.g., log-loss) and a complexity penalty for the trees
```
```
(controlling the number of leaves and leaf weights). It utilizes a second-order Taylor approximation of the loss
```
```
function to find optimal split points, which allows for highly efficient and accurate tree building, particularly on
```
sparse or structured tabular data.
3.8.4. Gradient Boosting
```
Gradient Boosting is a sequential ensemble technique that fits new weak learners (typically shallow decision trees)
```
```
to the negative gradient of the loss function [20]. At each iteration, the model computes the pseudo-residuals (the
```
```
difference between true and predicted values) and fits a new tree to these residuals. The predictions of the new
```
```
tree are then added to the ensemble, scaled by a learning rate (shrinkage parameter) to control the step size. This
```
iterative process allows the model to progressively minimize the loss function, capturing subtle nonlinear patterns
while the shallow tree depth prevents individual trees from overfitting.
3.8.5. Support Vector Machine
```
Support Vector Machine (SVM) is a margin-based classifier that seeks to find the optimal separating hyperplane
```
between classes [29]. The algorithm maximizes the margin—the distance between the hyperplane and the closest
```
data points from either class (the support vectors). For non-linearly separable data, a kernel trick (such as the
```
```
Radial Basis Function, K(xi, xj ) = exp(−γ||xi − xj ||2)) is used to implicitly map the input features into a
```
higher-dimensional space where a linear separating hyperplane can be found. SVM is highly effective in high-
```
dimensional spaces and robust to overfitting, especially when the margin parameter (C) is properly tuned.
```
3.8.6. Extra Trees
```
Extremely Randomized Trees (Extra Trees) is an ensemble method similar to Random Forest, but with an ad-
```
ditional layer of randomization [30]. While Random Forest selects the optimal split threshold for each feature
at a node, Extra Trees selects the split threshold completely at random for each candidate feature. This extreme
randomization further decorrelates the trees and reduces variance, often resulting in faster training times and com-
parable or superior generalization performance, particularly on noisy datasets.
