5. Discussion
5.1. Principal Findings
Four findings emerge from this study.
The first finding is that accuracy is inadequate under severe imbalance. Random Forest achieves high accuracy
but low recall, missing nearly two-thirds of at-risk employees. In retention-support contexts, this is operationally
unacceptable.
The second finding is that pipeline order matters. SMOTE applied inside cross-validation folds, rather than before
splitting, yields a substantial F1 gain. Feature engineering provides a further gain. This confirms that a leakage-
free protocol is not merely a methodological nicety, but changes substantive conclusions.
The third finding is that complex ensemble gains are not guaranteed. Contrary to the expectation that Stacking
would dominate, Logistic Regression achieved the best F1 and MCC. Statistical testing confirmed that the Stacking
```
Ensemble did not significantly outperform simpler models (DeLong p = 0.786). Correlation analysis shows tree-
```
based learners are highly correlated, explaining the lack of ensemble benefit.
The fourth finding is that subgroup fairness is severely compromised. The model achieves 0.00 recall for senior
```
employees (Job Level 3+), meaning it fails entirely to flag them for retention interventions.
```
17
5.2. Accuracy Versus Minority-Class Detection
```
Random Forest’s high accuracy reflects the majority class (83.88% base rate) and obscures its failure on the
```
minority class. This is not a tuning artifact, but the expected behavior of classifiers trained under severe imbalance.
PR-AUC is more informative than ROC-AUC in this regime [26]: PR-AUC varies significantly across models
against a 0.138 baseline, whereas ROC-AUC remains relatively stable.
5.3. Ensemble Learning and Model Complementarity
The analysis contradicts the common assumption that complex ensembles always win. Statistical comparison
```
between Stacking and the strongest baseline (Gradient Boosting) shows no significant difference in performance.
```
This is interpreted as evidence that for this small, highly imbalanced tabular dataset, the added complexity of
stacking does not justify its cost, and simpler models like Logistic Regression generalize better.
5.4. Objective-Function Sensitivity
The choice of tuning objective affects the precision-recall trade-off, but does not fundamentally change the ranking
of the best models. This demonstrates that optimization objectives must be chosen to match deployment priorities.
5.5. Threshold Optimization for Retention Decisions
The validation-selected threshold of 0.42 degraded holdout performance compared to the default 0.50 for the
Stacking model. This highlights the variance inherent in threshold selection on small datasets and suggests that
threshold optimization should be evaluated cautiously.
5.6. Business-Oriented Model Selection
The cost-sensitive framework confirms that Logistic Regression is the optimal model across all evaluated FN:FP
ratios, providing a robust, defensible choice for HR deployment.
5.7. Explainability and Predictive Features
Feature-importance analysis identifies overtime, marital status, and stock option level as features most strongly
associated with model predictions. Causal language is deliberately avoided: the model identifies predictive asso-
ciations, not causal drivers.
5.8. Empirical Performance Boundary
The observed accuracy boundary reflects the current feature set and experimental design. Substantial unexplained
variation remains, and this is identified as an empirical performance boundary rather than a theoretical ceiling.
5.9. Comparison with Previous Studies
The results are broadly consistent with prior work in terms of the importance of overtime and satisfaction, but
```
diverge regarding ensemble superiority. Govindarajan et al. [1] reported 95.0% accuracy with a hybrid model;
```
the highest observed accuracy in this study is 89.34%. This study provides a stricter benchmark by emphasizing
PR-AUC, recall, and statistical validation.
18
5.10. Practical Implications for HR Decision Support
```
The following workflow is recommended: employees are scored with the champion model (Logistic Regression);
```
```
the operating threshold is selected based on the organizational FN:FP cost ratio; top-ranked employees are routed
```
to HR review. However, given the 0.00 recall for senior employees, the model should not be deployed for that
subgroup without further feature engineering or data collection. The model should support, rather than replace,
human decision-making.
5.11. Limitations and Generalizability
Several limitations apply. First, the study is based on a single benchmark dataset. Second, the design is cross-
sectional. Third, external validity is uncertain. Fourth, SMOTE generates synthetic observations. Fifth, important
organizational and psychological variables are absent. Sixth, calibration is reported only for the champion model.
Seventh, model ranking depends on the operating threshold. Eighth, feature importance explains model behavior,
not causal relationships. Ninth, the severe subgroup disparity for senior employees means the model is not fair or
reliable across all populations. Tenth, organizational conditions change over time.
6. Conclusion
This research presented a methodologically rigorous machine learning pipeline for employee attrition prediction
using the IBM HR Analytics dataset. A leakage-free protocol was designed in which SMOTE, feature selection,
and hyperparameter tuning are embedded inside the cross-validation loop, eliminating synthetic-sample leakage.
Baseline model performance was reported using repeated stratified 5-fold cross-validation with a full metric suite.
```
Surprisingly, Logistic Regression emerged as the champion model (F1 = 52.41%, Recall = 53.52%), outper-
```
forming complex ensembles. Statistical model comparison using DeLong tests, paired bootstrap intervals, and
McNemar tests established that the Stacking Ensemble did not provide a statistically significant improvement
over simpler baselines. A cost-sensitive decision framework confirmed Logistic Regression as the optimal choice
across all FN:FP ratios. Threshold optimization revealed that validation-based threshold selection can occasion-
ally degrade holdout performance due to sampling variance. Calibration analysis confirmed reasonable calibration,
and Decision Curve Analysis demonstrated net benefit over default strategies.
Most critically, subgroup fairness analysis revealed a severe disparity: the model achieved a 0.00 recall for senior
```
employees (Job Level 3+), completely failing to identify them as at-risk. This highlights an urgent need for fairness
```
auditing before deploying automated retention systems.
Future work will proceed in five directions. First, temporal modeling will be pursued using longitudinal data.
Second, external validation will be conducted on multi-organization datasets. Third, SHAP-based per-employee
explainability will be integrated. Fourth, fairness-aware training will be investigated to address the severe sub-
group disparities identified here. Fifth, drift monitoring and online recalibration mechanisms will be developed to
maintain predictive reliability as organizational conditions change.
